        "sourceId": "st_croix-estate-saint-croix",
        "label": "Baint Croix; Island in northeastern corner of Caribbean Sea, 40 niiles southsoutheast of St4 ThOnlaS; St. Crolx being polltically included with Virgin Islands, under both Danish and American rule, and largest of said gruup. Area previously estirnnted at from 77 to 110 square milea; computed with precisfon by the author In 192%m d found to be 52, 432 acres, or 81. 93 square miles, to which may be added 196 acres, or 0. 31 square miles, -- 167 of 215 IS4 U. s. COAS'l' AND GfiODETK! $trRVEY for affjncent cays, makiog a total of $2. 24 square miles. The island is elongatM in form, atretching east-northeast west-southwest; extreme length, 23 nlfles; maximunl width, 6 miles. The hlghea summit, Mount Eagle, h&s an altitude of 1, 169 feel!. The island is phpiographically divided into thPee regions: West Oblong, Neck and East Triangle. There are two hill systems, built of Bluebeach; Central Slope and the Neck, of Marlstone. St. Croix is separated from St. Thomas by a deep of 1, 100 to 2, 450 fathoma. It rises steepto 600 to 800 fathoms from sea on northwest and west. Edge of submarine shelf, equal to island in area, is marked by 10 and 100 fathom ctwves, closely parallel. Northeast of the Neck, the shelf makes seaward; averaging 3 miles ofibhore, north and south, at Eastend; prolongrrtion, called Lang Bank, continuing east-northeast 10 miles. Shoals along entire south coast for 2 or 3 miles oflshore. Climate warm but congenial. Mean temperature for 28 years ranges from 78. 3' F. in February to 82\" in August. Normeast tradewind blows most of year; rainy season, August to December; average precipitation, 47. 56 inches. St. Croix has belonged successively to the Caribs, Spanish, English, French, Dutch, Danes, and Americans. The island was discovered by Columbus on his aecmd voyage, November 14, 1493. H e anchored, it i6 reported, near Saltriver Point to obtain water, and met Indians stmilar to the Carib1 of (fuadeloupe, with Arawak captives from Porto Rico. The natives called the island, Ayai or Agay. Here was fought the drat pitched battle in America between the red natives and the white invaders. The savage Caribs of b of h sexes desperately attacked the armorclad Spaniards, killing one with a poisoned arrow. Later, the slaveraiders from Hispanlola seized mRny natives, and a proclamation by Charles V ordered their extermination. In 60 years they had vanished. The Earl of Cumberland reported St. Croix a wilderness. Two colonies, one of Dutch, the other of French settlers, arrived in 1625 and were joined by French refugees. In 1646 the population was over 800. After a bloody clash with the English, in 1649, the Dutch retired to St. Eustatius and St. Martin. In 1660, 1, 200 Spaniards from Porto Rico in turn massacred the English, t h e remnant escaping to St. Kitts and Bermuda. The French governor, De Poincy, in 1651, sent De Vaugalan with 160 men from et. Ritts to evict the Spaniards. To clear out the fatal miasma, the French flred the dense forests, retreated aboarcl their ships, to observe the va5t conflkgration, lasting several months, then returned, planted the ground, and prosp&ed greatly. The soil was amazingly fertile, yielding abundant crops of tobacco, cotton, anatto, indigo, and sugar. Within 11 years the white population numbered 822. ' The history is varied and evatful. &The once flourishing but long Vanished French \"Plantages\" or settlements on St. Croll during the r e h e of the Knfgbta of Malts are briefly recorded in this work, with the aecertained or approximate loeatlon of each settlement, from the best acceeslble information. To facilitate reference, a Complete alphabetlc 1lsE of the entries follows : Alals, Andrleu, Andrln, Aubert, Baron, Bequel, Bllbas, Bluet, Boldrtc, Boucher, Boursbr, Branlant. Bretbd, Brevet, Brlant. Brunet, Buisronlere, Chausson, Coltou, Corterf, CourteWtte, Couturier, Crameni, Du Chwne, Du Rreme, Do Ramel, Du Val, Qaudri, Glrard, &be, Qranderle (2), Grange, Qulllarmet, Harang, Hedon, Hontum, Jacobins, La Chapelle, La Chesnqye, La Fleur, La Borert, La Granderle, La Roche, La Taille, La Verdure, Le Poida, L%scallue, Lesle, Lesperance, Le Vert, Margerln, Martel, Mauge, Mirieu, bhbacaeur, Mouchet, Murt, Musnler, Muze, Neuvllle, Pantaleon, Parialen, Feron, Peru, Boletevln, PoatotM. Potier. P o u W ( 8 ). Proven@, Qubet, €toriere, 8sQ8on, Btcnst, Solo, &tcmrle des Anglols, C'berlan, Yuel; besides the otffaial residence of Monsleur du Rols, Gouverneur de 1'1ale. The old French namcr of the bays and pointr can be aacertalned by conrultlng notices bepinnlnp with ''Arne '' and \" PoCts. \" -- 168 of 215 In 1651-1653, LOGSI I V of France transferred St. Crdn, St. Christopher, St. Bartholamew, and St Martin t4 the Kaigbts of W t a. They sold St. Crdr in 11866 to the French Wwt India Go. , whose pernicious regulations blighted the presperlty of the. w h y. In 1674, OR the dissolution of the compmy, the Island reverted to the Fremrh mown. la I S M, because of droughts, tke Frewh colmWs removed to Haiti and San Dombgo. In 1720 Llt. Crobx wa8 anhhpbiked. In 15'27 the French aptured seven lEnglieh veseel~, and twtmxwl ~ssession; but in 1783 they sold the island to King Christian Vi oF~l3enwark. for the Danifdh West India and Guinea CO. , for 760, oOO Iivres ($laO, W). In 1734, Oov. Errederik Motb took posseession, began a survey, subdivided the island into the esisting quarter^^, ' and porceled the land into oblong tracts, then called \"plantages, \" uow sstatee, each normelly measuring 3, 000 Danish feet (0. 585 mile) In length from north-northwest to south. southeaet, and 2, 000 Danish feet (0. 89 mile) In breadtb from eaet-northeast to westsouthwest, and containing 150 Danish acres cd 40, OOO Square feet to the acre. ' These estates were granted to b i g r a n t s from St. Eustatius, V Q g h GOrda, Tortola, e&. , who planted aUgt%i' cam, laid out the Eowns of Chrietianated aad B're&eriksted, and the sxisUslg network of good roads. (See mrps by Beck and Orholm. ) In 1751 the first census showed 122 cotton plantatiOR8 aad 120 gugar plantatioaa, worked by LQOO @lave& Inwrrections occurred In 1740 and 1768. the second resulting in the kllllng of 215 negroes. Slaves were bought for four rix dollars each, and in 1778 the 860 planters owned 30, OOO slaves. The whlh papolation eonsieted of Bnglish &'roan Nevis and Antigua, Dutch (these two owning the beet plantations), Irish German Momvbna, and 8 fi?, w Dan- In la1 the British m i d the island temporarily : amin in tW, under Sir Alex. Gochrane, but fluallJ reatore# it, lo 18Ur, by the treatrlof Peris. In 1848, the slaves won thelr freedom rnBar the hadersblp o i their \"GIeneral\" Boqrdeaux or Buddoe. I n 3. 878 occurred an men more dsvauhtlnp \"Lllborem' Riot, \" whea FrNer-g was sacked, p~engEatate h o u m burned, ruining the The St. CWiT Odonial OOUIAC~I adopted BD; impmved code of American laws, effmtbe alnw August 1, lO!Xk Chrletlensted and Frderiksted wch has a grammar achool and n junior hlgh gchool. Public ecbools am also lwated at YidlUnd, La Princesse, Peter's Rest, Lavallee and Diamond fi)stOta. The planters are intelligent lrnd progressire. #, W6 acres are i-31 farma (@ mr cePt d the area). The estate8 range in aUe from 146 h 2, 106 plan$era I Nwdsklr A'Kvnlba. Rcuc. NoTonhsfde A Quarter. N o r t w e B Vtpr. WwhndQuar. €Wnoe Quater. -- 169 of 215 acre8 : there has been recently a 2tmdency't0 coasolidation. Xach estate has, mirounding OF near the manse or estrrte house, its own Httle. tillage of stone hats, where live the families of the' employees. of the fields and mill, often comprising 100 persons, with stables f6t mlles dnd o&e&' Gattlr breeding fa subsidiary to can& iaising, l to provide work-oxen, tnanure; etc. Acreage in sugar (1917), 8, 684, yielding 84, 128 tons of cane, from which were manufactured 8, 149 'tons of mgnr, 90, 470 gallons of rum, 188, 000 galbns of molasses. Central Sugar Factory, where most of the cane i o ground, was established by