located the a?& of the stream in the Florida Straits and ofp Cape Hatteras, besides ascertaining many important facts pertaining to its origin and causes, temperature and salinity, direction and loclty and position, and the tidal e f f w in it of the sun and moon. His contributions t o a knowledge of the immutable laws controlling its movements brought withln the range of scientific prediction the puzzling fluctuation8 of this vast oceanic river, which pours through the Florida &rafts 90 billlon tons of warm water every hour. J u l y 1, 1802, after 20 years of meritorious service i n the grade of lieutenant, h e was advanced to that of lieutenant commander, and August 10, 1898, to that of commarider. Daring the Spanish-American War, he commanded the dynamite cruiser Vesuviua at the slege of Santiago de Cuba. Thereafter he was in charge of the equipment department at the Boston Navy Yard, and In 1901-2 W a s again afloat, commanding the U. 5. 9. Prairie. He was next detailed to duty in Washington as a member of the General Board and, assistant to the Chief of the Bureau of Navigation. November 10, 1902, he was promoted to the rank of captain. I n 1905 he became t h e Chief of Staff of the North Atlantic Fleet, and in 1908-9 served as Chief of the Bureau of Navigation. July 4, 1908, h e was made a rear admiral, and on December 15 was retired. In July, 1909, he was relieved from active duty. Admiral Pillsbury was a fellow of the American Association for the Advancement of Sclence, and a member of the American Academy of Arts and 209 -- 213 of 215 210 U. 6. COAST AND GEODETIC SURVEY Sciences. I n 1909, he accepted a place on the Eoard of Alanngers of the National Geographic Society, was vice president of the society from 1915 until 1919, was elected as its president in April, 1919, and served until his death. His Washington residelice was at 1755 Q Street, NW. He died, at the age of 73 years, on December 30, 1919. Among Admiral Pillsbury's bersonnl characteristics. none